// Asset budgets for the shipped model set (issue #868).
//
// Unlike the timing baselines, these numbers are machine-independent: they are
// bytes on disk and counts in authored TOML, so any checkout of this commit
// reproduces them exactly. That is why the tolerances here are tight — a drift
// is a real change to what a player downloads, never runner noise.
//
// Recorded at 52 .glb files, 228.5 MB total, largest 17.3 MB, 31 entity
// templates with a LOD ladder (four levels on 28 of them, three on the other
// three) and 9 naming a .glb with none.
//
// Re-recorded for issue #946, which added four uncommon and four rare asteroid
// types: 24 .glb files, 58.1 MB. The download total moves by MORE than that,
// and the difference is not #946's:
//
//   156.8 MB  the previous record (e87c871, 22 files)
//   170.4 MB  the tree #946 started from (28 files) — already +8.7%, past the
//             5% warn band, from remeshed commons and the dynasty_battleship
//             rig landing without a re-record
//   228.5 MB  this record (52 files)
//
// So of the +71.7 MB here, 58.1 MB is #946's content and 13.6 MB is drift that
// was already sitting in the tree. Adopting it here is deliberate — it was
// going to be re-discovered by whoever next moved this number — but it is NOT
// #946's cost, and the next person to ask "why is the download 228 MB" should
// read it as two answers rather than one.
//
// `assets.glb.without_lod` moved 12 → 9 in that same window and is an
// improvement, not a regression: ship_harrow_{destroyer,warhawk} and the
// three-weapon escort that then sat beside them gained ladders. Largest model
// (17,298,572) and ladder depth (4) did not move at all, which is the check
// that says the rest of this is content and not a collector that started
// reading the tree differently.
//
// NOT re-recorded for issue #954, which moved that escort out of the shipped
// fleet to `assets/entities/test/rng_coverage_lancer.toml`. `inventory` walks
// `assets/entities` at the top level only, so the template left the sample:
// templates carrying a ladder went 35 → 34. None of the four gated numbers can
// move for it — it authors no geometry of its own (it named
// `dynasty_battleship.glb`, which the destroyer and warhawk still name, so the
// byte total and the largest model are untouched), its own ladder was 3 deep so
// it never set the depth max, and it HAD a ladder so it was never one of the 9.
//
// NOT re-recorded for issue #947, which added the huge asteroid size class —
// and that is the point of it. The new size reuses the four common `.glb`
// files at a larger authored scale, so it ships sixteen small TOMLs and not one
// byte of geometry. Templates carrying a ladder went 31 → 35 (the sample count
// behind `assets.lod.levels`, which this baseline gates on `max`, not on how
// many templates were sampled), and all four gated numbers — 228,463,236
// total, 17,298,572 largest, 9 without a ladder, ladder depth 4 — are unchanged
// to the byte. A size class that moved the download total would be one that
// added geometry; this gate staying green is the evidence it did not.
//
// Re-recorded for commit b78bf9ff, which rebuilt all 22 shipped models on a
// 15/100/400 LOD ladder and re-optimised every base to <=8 MB. That rebuild —
// not any change in how the collector reads the tree — moves four of the five
// tracked numbers:
//
//   .glb files                  52 -> 75
//   assets.glb.bytes (largest)  17,298,572 (alliance_destroyer) -> 8,319,504 (dynasty_cruiser)
//   assets.glb.total.bytes      228,463,236 -> 232,630,348
//   assets.glb.without_lod      9 -> 0
//   assets.lod.levels           4 -> 4  (unchanged)
//
// The single largest model more than halved and every shipped model now carries
// a ladder (`without_lod` fell to zero), even as the file count and the download
// total edged up: the rebuild spread the same content across more, smaller
// levels, and no base now exceeds 8 MB. This re-record is captured locally under
// the same rule the header already invokes below — every number is `stat` on the
// committed tree, not a runner timing.
//
// The huge asteroid x3 band restore (issue #947: 45/300/1200) committed
// ALONGSIDE this re-record changes no `.glb` — it rewrites only `max_distance`
// switch metadata in the four `.huge.toml` sidecars — so this capture is
// byte-identical whether or not that restore is applied. The "#947 moved no
// gated number" note above still holds: b78bf9ff moved them, the band restore
// did not.
//
// Re-recorded after issues #998-#1001 at 66 runtime GLBs and 175,619,464 bytes.
// The collector no longer charges the nine `.remesh.glb` generator
// intermediates to a visitor: Trunk publishes them because it copies the whole
// assets directory, but no runtime sidecar requests them. The remaining drop
// comes from tuned generated LODs, the reduced uncommon-4 base, and the two
// courier diffuse maps moving from 2048 to 1024. Lower-is-better drift now
// passes directionally, but adopting the reduction makes the tighter number
// the budget future growth is compared against.
//
// These are a record, not a target. 175.6 MB of models is still a great deal
// for a browser game to fetch, and the point of committing it is that the
// number is visible in every CI run and moves in a diff someone reads.
//
// THIS SCENARIO GATES (issue #905). The CI perf job runs its report with
// --gate, so a fail here turns the run red. It earned that by being a function
// of the checkout rather than of the host: the runner's own capture of e87c871
// compared at +0.0% drift on every metric. src/perf/mod.rs carries the rule,
// and why the timing scenarios have not earned it.
//
// Provenance: this record was captured locally rather than adopted from a CI
// perf-capture artifact, which the gating rule in src/perf/mod.rs allows for
// this scenario and only this one — condition (2) reads "for a metric that
// passes (1) that is every machine", and (1) holds here because every number
// below is `stat` on a committed tree or a count in authored TOML. The runner
// confirms it on the first perf job of this commit. A timing baseline could
// not honestly be recorded this way.
//
// Metric by metric, since the tolerances now decide a build:
//
// - assets.glb.total.bytes — all runtime GLBs on disk, excluding build-only
//   `.remesh.glb` intermediates. Warns when growth exceeds 5%; reductions pass.
// - assets.glb.bytes — the single largest model, the one that dominates a cold
//   load.
// - assets.glb.without_lod — LOD coverage, counted as templates with no ladder
//   at all. A rise is "someone changed the LOD story, look at it".
// - assets.lod.levels — how deep the ladders that exist go. A deeper ladder is
//   an improvement, so its fail tolerance is deliberately wide: adding a fifth
//   level should read as a warning, never as a red build.
//
// Triangle and texture counts are not here. They are the `assets-mesh`
// scenario, read through Bevy's own loader rather than by parsing the GLB a
// second way — see src/perf/mesh.rs.
//
// Commentary belongs in this header: `phoenix-perf adopt` regenerates the RON
// value below from a capture, so a comment written inside it would be deleted
// by the next recording. See src/perf/baseline.rs.
//
// ── Recorded from a capture ─────────────────────────────────
// Written by `phoenix-perf adopt` from a static-inventory capture of scenario "assets".
// build: dev   device: (unrecorded)
// rev:   (unrecorded)
//
// Edit the prose ABOVE this block; re-recording rewrites from here down, and
// carries every statistic and tolerance over unchanged. The numbers are the
// capture's; the tolerances are yours.
(
    scenario: "assets",
    expectations: {
        "assets.glb.bytes": (
            unit: bytes,
            statistic: max,
            expected: 8319504.0,
            tolerance: (
                warn: 0.1,
                fail: 0.5,
            ),
        ),
        "assets.glb.total.bytes": (
            unit: bytes,
            statistic: max,
            expected: 175619464.0,
            tolerance: (
                warn: 0.05,
                fail: 0.25,
            ),
        ),
        "assets.glb.without_lod": (
            unit: count,
            statistic: max,
            expected: 0.0,
            tolerance: (
                warn: 0.25,
                fail: 1.0,
            ),
        ),
        "assets.lod.levels": (
            unit: count,
            statistic: max,
            expected: 4.0,
            tolerance: (
                warn: 0.25,
                fail: 2.0,
            ),
        ),
    },
)
